    "notes": "PROVISIONAL. The load is fixed at 50 lb per hand for everyone, so the sex gap in the anchors is wider than it would be on a bodyweight-relative carry: a fixed load is a smaller fraction of a heavier person's strength. The anchors put a median 42-year-old somewhere around two and a half minutes of walking before grip failure. The 0.5 coefficient of variation is wide on purpose: distance to grip failure has a heavy right tail. Surface and route matter more here than on any other test in the battery, so protocol discipline beats norm precision. Older cohorts will bunch near zero at a fixed load - that is real, and it is the same shape the pull-up file has. The decline per decade is steeper than the bodyweight-relative version used to be (0.22 against 0.15), because a fixed load does not shrink with the athlete: 100 lb total is a moderate carry at 35 and close to a maximal one at 80.",
